ask you first as a public health  specialist when you see all of these  people out on the streets in close  contact in the middle of a pandemic  knowing the novel coronavirus has not  gone away how much does it concern you I  think the fact that people are willing  to go out and speak about human rights  issues even though that they know that  there is a cove at 19 pandemic but they  feel that  passionately about it I think deeply  moves me in fact I think overall is the  public health position I can say that it  is important as you know dr. Teresa Tam  and others said to wear personal  protective equipment so wearing a mask  using sanitizer but what I see as a  black woman and physician is people  coming together to address a major issue  that we have in Canada which is anti  black racism which is in fact been  contributing to worse health outcomes  for black people for century an anti  black racism traces back to our history  of slavery of having segregation here  but also just a series of issues around  community injustice Canada is not the  United States we do have a different  history here we like to pride ourselves  on tolerance and diversity as a black  woman and a black physician explain to  us your experience your lived experience  in this country and working as a doctor  in this country my lived experience and  born and raised in Montreal so that's  where I'm originally from living in  Toronto now as a child I experienced  racism you know in the schoolyard being  called the n-word and things like that  but what I think was most painful is  where teachers made assumptions about my  intelligence at a very young age and I  saw it happen you know time and time  again to my classmate so that's what I  grew up seeing and then becoming  position and then looking at the data  from 1996 to 2006 in Montreal a black  person with a graduate degree had pretty  much the same unemployment rate as a non  black high school dropout I live  I saw the people who went and got an  education did everything that they  needed to do to advance and live that  Canadian dream and be denied  opportunities and I saw the impact that  it had on my ears on young people seeing  you know our parents are telling us to  go to school but they're not getting the  opportunities and so you know now when I  look at the research and the data and I  see how across so many factors that  affect health whether it's income or  it's education or it's our labor force  that people of African descent are  denied opportunities really just based  on the color of our skin even despite  education what is really painful is that  the United Nations flagged that in  Canada institutionalized anti black  racism is an issue here they flagged  that in 2017 so it's not just my opinion  it has actually been demonstrated to  research who report and even with the  United Nations expressing their grave  concern for people of African descent  here in Canada so when you hear people  say that there is no systemic racism in  Canada what do you think when you hear  that well I think it's akin to where you  might have people who are of a dominant  group so let's say men think they don't  think there is any sexism when you're a  part of the group that is discriminated  against you are most likely to know  where there is discrimination I think in  Canada we have that denial we aspire to  that dream of you know a multicultural  society where everybody has equal  opportunity and I think it would be  beautiful if we get there I think we all  aspire to that but we have to face the  fact that we are not there so we can  take the right steps to get there you're  president of the black physicians of  Ontario you also train public health  officials what's the biggest  misperception among the public and  public officials about the connection  between background racial background  ethnic background and health outcomes I  think there are a few but I think that  one of the biggest ones that exists in  medicine is assuming  that race and genetics are tied together  so that where you do see differences  between race so let the rates of  hypertension one would it presumes that  it's due to and to genetic differences  but in fact we know that there's no  genetic or biological differences  between the races race is a social  construct that is used to create social  barriers and stressors that then impact  health so the concept of race is only  real insofar that we as a society  categorize people that way and treat  people based on that but it is not  biological let's talk about race-based  data we don't collect it in Canada  though some cities are now starting to  do that you believe it is vital can you  explain why we're cold in nineteen but  even Cryer Cove in nineteen to be able  to take the appropriate measures to  address health issues you need a clear  picture of what's going on at a  population level so that's kind of  standard public health approach so  that's why we collect information on  gender or where people live sometimes  it's their postal code just to get a  sense of you know risk factors things  that make people more vulnerable the  other reason that you want to collect  the data is that if you do have an  intervention if you put resources  towards prevention or addressing an  issue you want to know that it is  actually improved and is working on the  other hand for a lot of racialized and  including indigenous communities there  is a certain amount of mistrust with  having you know government leaders  collect this information and interpret  it in a way that can sting the thais  communities so we know that in Canada  lots of data has been collected on  indigenous communities and used to  stigmatize them we know that in the  United States lots of data has been used  in describing African American  communities and used to stigmatize and  pathologize that to make it seem like  something is wrong with that community  instead of something's wrong with the  society that put the community in that  situation that's why there's been a  hesitancy to collect it race-based data  yes on both ends so I think for some  people not knowing what to do or not  wanting to deal with the console  quences of understanding what the racial  disparities are and then also  communities being concerned that it  would be used against them so on both  ends and so my position is aligned with  the Canadian Public Health Association  in which they have recommended the  culturally safe collection of race-based  data so that again it's not just about  how do we fix these communities but it's  like how do we address the factors in  society that that led to certain  communities being predisposed to to  having higher rate of koban 19:00  infection
 

